# PhilipHazel/pcre2. testinput1 (Perl-compatible) and testinput2
# (PCRE2-specific), from the release that matches the pcre2test on this
# machine: 10.46.
#
# And src/pcre2.h.in, the public header, because tools/oracle/pcre2_match.c
# links the installed libpcre2-8 and Debian 13 ships libpcre2-8.so.0 without
# the -dev package's pcre2.h. It is a configure template whose only open
# substitutions are four version macros; the Makefile fills them from this
# ref, so the header, the installed library and the imported testinput files
# are one version or the build is wrong in a way that says so.
#
# Why a driver at all when pcre2test is here: pcre2test reports the matched
# *text* rather than byte offsets, and omits a trailing group that did not
# participate rather than naming it. "Which span did group two get" is most
# of what a match comparison asks and is not something it says.
#
# A defect in 10.46, found by tools/oracle/perl_diff.py and confirmed against
# pcre2test itself, so it is the library and not the driver. A pattern
# holding both a lookbehind and a `(?[...])` extended class whose body uses
# an operator - `|`, `&`, `-`, `^` or `!` - fails to compile:
#
#   pcre2test <<< '/(?<=a)(?[ [a] | [b] ])/'
#   # Failed: error 170 at offset 0: internal error: unknown meta code in
#   # check_lookbehinds()
#
# A lookahead does not do it; an extended class with no operator does not do
# it; the order of the two does not matter. This library compiles those
# patterns and matches them, so the differential counts them as a known pcre2
# defect and prints the count. Searched the tracker rather than reporting
# anything, the way the perl entry below was handled.
#
# A second defect in 10.46, found by tools/oracle/script_run_diff.py: a
# script run may mix Han with *two* of its companion scripts, which
# pcre2's own manual says it may not. pcre2unicode's "Script Runs":
#
#   a script run may contain a mixture of Hiragana, Katakana, and Han, or a
#   mixture of Hangul and Han, or a mixture of Bopomofo and Han, but not,
#   for example, a mixture of Hangul and Bopomofo and Han
#
# and pcre2test matches that last one:
#
#   pcre2test <<< $'/(*UTF)^(*sr:.+)$/\n    \xe6\xbc\xa2\xed\x95\x9c\xe3\x84\x85'
#   #  0: (the whole subject)
#
# All twenty two- and three-way combinations of U+6F22 (Han), U+304B
# (Hiragana), U+30AB (Katakana), U+D55C (Hangul) and U+3105 (Bopomofo) were
# put to pcre2_match and to perl_match.pl. The two agree on fourteen -
# including Hiragana with Hangul, which both refuse, so it is not that the
# presence of Han lets anything through - and differ on exactly the six
# that mix two families. perl 5.40.1 refuses all six, which is UTS #39
# section 5.1 and what this library implements.
pcre2 pcre2-10.46

# Perl/perl5. t/re/re_tests, from the release matching the perl on this
# machine: 5.40.1.
#
# Which is to say this pin was not chosen - it followed what Debian 13 ships
# (5.40.1-6+deb13u1, a current security build, not a stale one). Worth
# knowing before raising it, checked 2026-09-20:
#
#   - 5.40.1 carries UCD 15.0.0. These tables are 17.0.0, so three
#     segmentation rules exist here that this perl has never seen: GB9c
#     (Unicode 15.1), LB15c (16) and LB21a's HH class (17). Forty-six rows of
#     tests/data/vectors/perl/boundaries.rxt are excluded for that reason,
#     each named in tools/corpus/make_boundary_vectors.py.
#   - 5.44.0 is the current stable release and carries UCD 17.0.0 - an exact
#     match with tools/unicode/UCD_VERSION. Raising the pin to it would
#     retire those forty-six exclusions.
#   - The direction matters more than the distance. An oracle *behind* the
#     tables is harmless and documented: the newer rules simply do not fire
#     for it. An oracle *ahead* of them would disagree across the whole
#     corpus, which is what documentation/unicode.md section 1 warns about.
#     So if this moves, UCD_VERSION moves with it or before it, never after.
#
# A second reason, found 2026-09-20 and unrelated to Unicode: 5.40.1 carries a
# known branch reset defect. Perl/perl5#24577, "Regex branch reset (?|...)
# fails with backrefs" - a regression introduced in 5.38, present through
# 5.44, closed 2026-07-22. Its own reproducer on this machine gives the wrong
# answer:
#
#   perl -e 'print "abb" =~ s{^(?|(a)|(b)) (?|(a)|(b)) ((\2)*)}{${&}z}xr'
#   # abzb here; abbz on 5.36 and earlier
#
# The same family shows up in the conditional, where perl contradicts its own
# capture report:
#
#   perl -e 'print "b" =~ /(?|(a)|(b))/ ? "g1=$1" : "no"'   # g1=b
#   perl -e 'print "bx" =~ /(?|(a)|(b))(?(1)x|y)/ ? 1 : 0'  # 0
#   perl -e 'print "by" =~ /(?|(a)|(b))(?(1)x|y)/ ? 1 : 0'  # 1
#
# Group 1 is "b", and the conditional testing it takes the false arm. One
# interpreter calls the same group set and unset in two lines.
#
# pcre2test 10.46 takes "bx", and so does this library. Two rows of
# tests/data/vectors/known-gaps.txt are the named form of that question, are
# categorised there as `reference-defect` rather than as gaps, and say to
# re-check when this pin moves. Searched the tracker rather than reporting
# anything; #24577 was already there and already fixed.
#
# The upstream record, confirmed 2026-09-22:
#   issue  Perl/perl5#24577 "Regex 'branch reset' (?|...) fails with backrefs"
#          present 5.38 through 5.44, bisected to commit fe5492d, closed
#   fix    Perl/perl5#24588 "regexec.c - remove unnecessary logical_to_parno[]
#          mapping", merged to blead 2026-07-22 as
#          8f0cfc33fc3fabc0d491f71b765b0937d245239c
#
# The fix names the cause, which is why the conditional and the backreference
# are one bug: a branch reset group's logical number was mapped to its real
# one twice, in regcomp.c and again in regexec.c, so everything that reads
# such a group *by number* reads the wrong one. The issue's own case
# reproduces on the pinned perl here:
#
#   perl -e 'print "abb" =~ s{^(?|(a)|(b)) (?|(a)|(b)) ((\2)*)}{${&}z}xr'
#
# prints abzb on 5.40.1 and printed abbz on 5.36.
perl v5.40.1

# bminor/glibc (the git mirror of sourceware's glibc). posix/rxspencer/tests,
# Henry Spencer's regular expression test set, which is what plan.md's WP-25
# names. The ref matches the glibc on this machine - Debian 13 ships 2.41 -
# because glibc is also the *oracle* for these vectors, and a corpus imported
# from one version and answered by another measures the difference between
# them rather than the library.
#
#   ldd --version   # ldd (Debian GLIBC 2.41-12+deb13u4) 2.41
#
# Two things about POSIX make the oracle matter more here than elsewhere.
# There is no reference *binary* to compare against the way pcre2test and
# perl are compared: glibc's regcomp/regexec is reached through C, which is
# what tools/oracle/posix_match.c exists to do. And POSIX leaves more to the
# implementation than either of the other two standards - the submatch rules
# in particular - so "what POSIX says" and "what glibc does" are not always
# the same sentence, and the vectors record the second.
glibc glibc-2.41

# musl. src/regex only - regcomp.c, regexec.c, tre-mem.c and tre.h - which
# tools/oracle/musl_match.c compiles into an oracle binary. v1.2.6 is the
# current release; there is no musl on this machine to match, which is the
# point of fetching it.
#
# Why a second POSIX oracle at all. glibc's regcomp is GNU: it accepts `\|`,
# `\+`, `\w` and `\<` in a BRE and defines a great deal that POSIX leaves
# undefined, so it can be asked what GNU does and cannot be asked what POSIX
# does. The two posix-* rows of the spec table were built from the standard
# and have never been compared against anything. musl's regex is descended
# from Ville Laurikari's TRE, a different implementation lineage entirely,
# which is what makes it a second *opinion* rather than a second spelling of
# the first - and TRE is also the tagged-transition algorithm plan.md's
# WP-26 names, so the same fetch is a working reference for that package.
#
# What this oracle is *not*. It is musl's regex hosted on glibc, not musl.
# The two translation units are compiled against glibc's headers and linked
# against glibc's mbtowc, iswctype and towlower, so three limits follow, all
# of them enforced or declared rather than hoped for:
#
#   - Comparisons are ASCII. In the C locale glibc's mbtowc refuses bytes
#     >= 0x80 where musl's would decode them as UTF-8, so a non-ASCII
#     subject would measure the host libc and not the regex. The driver
#     declines one rather than answering it.
#   - There is no REG_STARTEND, which is a glibc extension, so a subject
#     containing NUL cannot be expressed and is declined too. glibc's
#     driver can ask that question and this one cannot.
#   - Two limits come from musl's own include/limits.h on the command line,
#     because glibc's header does not define them: CHARCLASS_NAME_MAX 14 and
#     RE_DUP_MAX 255. Using glibc's RE_DUP_MAX (0x7fff) would have made this
#     oracle accept `a{1000}`, which musl refuses.
#
# And it is not the standard. musl is one more implementation with its own
# reading; where it and glibc disagree, that disagreement is the finding.
#
# Licence. musl is MIT, and this is the only entry here whose contents are
# compiled rather than read. Nothing of musl's is committed, nothing of it is
# installed, and musl_match is a development tool that `all` does not build -
# so no part of it reaches the shipped library or its package. If that ever
# stops being true, the MIT notice has to travel with it.
musl v1.2.6

# A defect in Node v22.23.2 / V8 12.4.254.21, found at seed 4009 of the
# replacement soak and minimised to one pattern with no alternation in it:
#
#   /(?:(?=a)a)*\B../u  over "\nabc" from offset 1
#
# is 1-4 on the *first* execution in a fresh process and **no match** on
# every execution after it. `--regexp-interpret-all` gives 1-4 always;
# `--no-regexp-tier-up`, which compiles immediately, gives no match always.
# So V8's regexp interpreter and its compiled code disagree, and the tier-up
# between them is the whole of it.
#
# 1-4 is the right answer - pcre2test 10.46 and perl 5.40.1 both give it,
# and so does this library - so the compiled path is the wrong one.
#
# The reason it matters more than one wrong answer: without the flag the
# oracle's answer to a row depends on how many rows ran before it, so the
# same case can pass or fail according to where it lands in a batch. A
# differential whose reference is order-dependent is not measuring what it
# says it measures. Every generator here asks Node through
# tools/oracle/node_runner.py, which adds the flag and refuses to run
# without it rather than falling back to a quietly order-dependent oracle.
